/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Modern History (batch z) */
WB.L("irish-great-famine", "history", "beginner", "Ireland's Great Hunger, 1845–1852",
  "A microscopic organism destroyed one crop, and within a few years Ireland had lost a quarter of its people. The blight was natural. The scale of the disaster was not.",
  [
    ["A crop and its killer", "Ireland's poorest depended almost entirely on one fragile potato crop, so when blight struck, there was nothing to fall back on.",
      "By the 1840s Ireland had about eight and a half million people, many renting tiny plots from landlords. Wheat, oats, cattle and butter were grown for rent and sale, but the poorest lived on potatoes. An acre could feed a family for much of a year, and a labourer might eat several kilograms a day. Many relied on one high-yielding but vulnerable variety, the ‘lumper’.\n\nIn 1845 Phytophthora infestans, a fungus-like water mould probably from the Americas, arrived. Leaves blackened and potatoes rotted in the ground. Roughly a third to a half of the 1845 crop was lost, and 1846 was worse. Other countries had blight too, but nowhere else was so large a share of people so dependent on the crop."],
    ["Relief, and its limits", "Government relief existed but was shaped by a belief that the market and local taxes should carry the burden.",
      "Prime Minister Robert Peel's government quietly bought American maize in 1845 to sell cheaply. From 1846 Lord John Russell's government leaned on the idea that the state should not interfere with trade. The starving were offered work on public projects such as roads, often for wages too low to buy food, and sometimes on roads that led nowhere.\n\nIn 1847 soup kitchens briefly fed around three million people a day, and they worked. Yet they were wound down within months, and responsibility shifted to workhouses and local property taxes. The Gregory clause denied relief to anyone holding more than a quarter acre, forcing many to give up their homes to qualify."],
    ["Food leaving a hungry land", "Ireland kept exporting food during the famine, but historians see the deeper failure as one of political choices, not supply.",
      "One of the most painful facts of the famine is that grain, cattle and butter kept leaving Ireland while people starved. Landlords needed rent, farmers sold crops to pay it, and armed guards sometimes escorted food convoys past hungry crowds.\n\nHistorians debate how much difference keeping that food would have made. From 1847 more grain was coming in than going out, and the loss of the potato was too vast for exports alone to fill. But most agree the deeper problem was choice, not supply. A wealthy empire could have kept people alive and instead treated the crisis as a chance to reform Irish agriculture. Charles Trevelyan, a senior Treasury official, came to symbolise that outlook."],
    ["Fever, eviction and death", "Most famine victims died of diseases that spread among weakened, homeless people, and about a million died in all.",
      "Hunger made people vulnerable, and disease did much of the killing. Typhus, relapsing fever and dysentery swept through crowded workhouses, roadsides and cabins. Scurvy appeared as diets collapsed.\n\nLandlords, facing unpaid rents and rising local taxes, evicted hundreds of thousands of tenants, often tearing down cottages so families could not return. Whole communities in the west, in counties such as Mayo, Galway and Cork, were devastated. Around one million people died between 1845 and 1852, though exact numbers are uncertain because so many deaths went unrecorded. Irish speakers called it An Gorta Mór, the Great Hunger. The worst year, 1847, became known as Black ’47, and mass graves from that time are still being identified."],
    ["The great scattering", "Over a million people emigrated, Ireland's population kept falling for decades, and the famine reshaped Irish identity worldwide.",
      "For many families the only hope was to leave. Well over a million emigrated, mostly to Britain, the United States and Canada, some on overcrowded ‘coffin ships’. At the quarantine station on Grosse Île in Canada, thousands died of typhus in 1847 alone. Between 1848 and 1850 an assisted scheme brought more than four thousand Irish orphan girls to Sydney, Melbourne and Adelaide.\n\nIreland's population kept falling for decades and the island still holds fewer people than before the famine. The Irish language declined, bitterness towards British rule hardened, and Irish communities abroad kept the memory alive. In 1847 the Choctaw Nation, recently forced from their own lands, sent money to help, honoured today by a sculpture in County Cork. Most historians see a natural disaster made far deadlier by poverty, a harsh land system and government decisions."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why were so many Irish people exposed when the blight struck?", ["Ireland had never grown potatoes before","The poorest relied almost entirely on one potato crop","Ireland imported nearly all of its potatoes","Potatoes were reserved for the landlords"], 1, "With one fragile crop as their main food, the poorest had nothing to fall back on when it failed."],
    [1, "Why did government relief fall short?", ["Free-trade ideas left the burden on workhouses and local taxes","The Irish people refused all offers of aid","Britain had no means of buying any food","Relief was never attempted by anyone"], 0, "Soup kitchens worked but were closed within months, and the policy relied on markets and local taxes."],
    [2, "What do most historians conclude about food exports during the famine?", ["Exports alone caused the whole famine","Exports could not fill the gap, but choices made it worse","Exports had stopped entirely by early 1846","Exports were seized by the army before sale"], 1, "The deeper problem was political choice, not a lack of supply."],
    [3, "What killed most famine victims?", ["Violence between tenants and landlords","Freezing winters in the western counties","Typhus and dysentery spreading among weakened people","Shipwrecks on the crossing to Canada"], 2, "Hunger weakened people, and fevers and dysentery spread through workhouses, roadsides and cabins."],
    [4, "What happened to Ireland's population after the famine?", ["It recovered fully within about a decade","It kept falling for decades and is still lower","It doubled because of immigration","It stayed the same throughout the century"], 1, "Emigration became a habit that lasted generations."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A mould ruined one crop. Why did so many Irish people die?"});

WB.L("christmas-truce-1914", "history", "beginner", "The Christmas Truce of 1914",
  "Five months into the most industrial war yet fought, enemy soldiers climbed out of their trenches, shook hands and sang carols together. Then they went back to killing each other.",
  [
    ["Live and let live", "Soldiers in trenches only metres apart began informal understandings to hold fire, which made the Christmas truce possible.",
      "In August 1914 many expected the war to be over by Christmas. Instead, the German advance was stopped at the Marne and both armies dug in. By winter trenches ran from the Belgian coast to the Swiss border, sometimes only fifty to a few hundred metres apart. Men lived in freezing water among rats and the unburied dead. Yet the closeness let them hear the enemy talking, coughing and singing.\n\nSmall unofficial understandings grew. Each side might spare the other at breakfast, or let men fetch water or bring in wounded. Historians call it ‘live and let live’. In December Pope Benedict XV called for a Christmas pause, but governments ignored him."],
    ["Candles on the parapet", "The truce began with ordinary soldiers, not leaders, as German trenches lit candles and British soldiers answered in song.",
      "On Christmas Eve 1914, British soldiers in several sectors were astonished to see lights along the German trenches. German troops had placed small Christmas trees, decorated with candles, on their parapets. Then came singing, including ‘Stille Nacht’, Silent Night. The British replied with their own carols, and each side applauded the other across no man's land.\n\nShouted conversations followed, helped by Germans who had worked in Britain and spoke English. A few brave men walked out unarmed. When nobody fired, others followed. By Christmas morning, along stretches of the front, men who had been trying to kill each other days earlier stood together in the frozen mud."],
    ["Gifts, graves and football", "Soldiers swapped gifts, buried their dead together and probably kicked balls about, though no organised match is proven.",
      "Letters home describe an extraordinary day. Men exchanged cigarettes, chocolate, plum puddings, buttons and badges, and showed each other photographs of their families. Barbers cut enemy hair. Most importantly, both sides used the calm to recover and bury the bodies lying between the lines, sometimes with joint services.\n\nFootball is the most famous part of the story. Several soldiers wrote of kickabouts with the enemy, sometimes with a makeshift ball, and these accounts seem genuine. But a single organised match with a final score is much harder to prove. Historians think informal games happened in a few places, not one grand fixture across the front."],
    ["Not everywhere, not for everyone", "The truce was patchy and brief, and in many places the shooting never stopped.",
      "It is easy to imagine the whole front falling silent, but that did not happen. The truce was strongest where British and German troops faced each other, especially around Ypres in Belgium. It occurred in some French and Belgian sectors too, but less often, perhaps because those soldiers were fighting on their own invaded soil.\n\nElsewhere fighting continued and men were killed on Christmas Day. Some truces lasted only hours, others until New Year or beyond. By some estimates around one hundred thousand soldiers took part, but nobody knows the true figure. Many soldiers felt uneasy, some officers were furious, and not every man who wrote home that week described peace."],
    ["Never again", "Commanders banned fraternisation to stop a repeat, but the truce remains proof that shared humanity can survive even total war.",
      "High commands saw the truce as a threat to discipline. If soldiers came to like their enemies, how could they be ordered to kill them? In following years strict orders forbade fraternisation, and bombardments were sometimes scheduled for Christmas. Small local pauses still happened in 1915, but nothing like 1914. The war grew more brutal, with poison gas, the Somme and Verdun.\n\nWhen British newspapers printed soldiers' letters and photographs in January 1915, readers were amazed. Some retellings are romanticised, but the core is well documented in hundreds of letters and diaries. The truce did not shorten the war. Its power lies in an uncomfortable question: if the men in the trenches could stop, why couldn't those in charge?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the ‘live and let live’ system?", ["An official peace treaty between the armies","Informal habits of holding fire on quiet sectors","A medical programme for wounded soldiers","A prisoner exchange scheme at Christmas"], 1, "Close trenches and shared misery led soldiers to quietly ease the killing, long before the famous truce."],
    [1, "Who started the Christmas truce?", ["Ordinary soldiers who lit candles, sang and walked out unarmed","Generals who signed a Christmas ceasefire","The governments after the Pope's appeal","Chaplains who were ordered to negotiate"], 0, "The truce came from soldiers acting on their own, not from leaders."],
    [2, "What do historians conclude about football during the truce?", ["There was one official match with a recorded score","Informal kickabouts probably happened in a few places","No football was ever played","Football was banned by the Pope"], 1, "Soldiers' letters support casual games, but a single organised match is hard to prove."],
    [3, "Which statement about the truce is accurate?", ["It covered the whole front until the war ended","It occurred only on the French side","It was patchy, and fighting continued in some places","It was ordered by senior British officers"], 2, "The truce was strongest between British and German troops around Ypres, but far from universal."],
    [4, "Why did commanders work to prevent another truce?", ["Soldiers who befriended the enemy might refuse to fight","The winter weather had finally improved","Newspapers had banned all stories about it","The Pope had forbidden any repeat"], 0, "Armies depend on soldiers seeing the other side as an enemy, so fraternisation was forbidden."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In 1914, enemies climbed out of their trenches on Christmas. How did it start?"});

WB.L("titanic-sinking", "history", "beginner", "The Titanic: one night that changed the sea",
  "On a calm, moonless night in April 1912, the largest ship afloat struck an iceberg and sank in under three hours. The rules that keep ships safe today were written in its wake.",
  [
    ["Big, luxurious, ‘unsinkable’", "Titanic was built for size and comfort, and the claim that it was advertised as unsinkable is mostly a later exaggeration.",
      "RMS Titanic was built in Belfast by Harland and Wolff for the White Star Line and launched in 1911. About 269 metres long, it was briefly the largest ship in the world. White Star competed with Cunard on size and luxury, not speed. First class had a swimming pool, a gymnasium and Turkish baths. The hull was divided into sixteen watertight compartments, and the ship could float with any two flooded, or the first four.\n\nA trade journal called such ships ‘practically unsinkable’, but it was not a central advertising slogan. The boldest versions of the claim spread after the disaster. Still, confidence in modern ships was real, and it contributed to too few lifeboats and ignored ice warnings."],
    ["Ice warnings", "Titanic received several ice warnings yet kept up a high speed through a calm, moonless night.",
      "Titanic left Southampton on 10 April 1912, called at Cherbourg and Queenstown, and headed for New York with over two thousand people aboard. On 14 April the Marconi wireless operators received several warnings about ice ahead. Not all reached the bridge, partly because the operators were busy sending passengers' private messages.\n\nCaptain Edward Smith altered course slightly but kept the ship at about 22 knots, close to its top speed, as was normal practice then. The sea was flat calm with no moon, so no waves broke around the base of icebergs to reveal them. The lookouts in the crow's nest had no binoculars."],
    ["Collision and sinking", "The iceberg opened too many compartments, and water spilled from one to the next until Titanic sank in about two hours and forty minutes.",
      "At about 11.40 pm on 14 April, lookout Frederick Fleet spotted an iceberg dead ahead. The officer on watch ordered a hard turn, but too late. The iceberg scraped along the starboard side below the waterline, buckling plates and popping rivets along a long stretch of hull. It was not one huge gash but a series of openings.\n\nWater poured into the first five or six compartments, more than the design could survive. Thomas Andrews, the ship's designer, who was aboard, told the captain the ship would sink. As the bow went down, water spilled over the tops of the bulkheads from one compartment into the next. At about 2.20 am on 15 April, Titanic broke apart and sank."],
    ["Not enough boats", "Titanic carried lifeboats for only about half of those aboard, and the nearest ship never came.",
      "Titanic carried twenty lifeboats with room for about 1,178 people. That exceeded the outdated British rules, which were based on a ship's weight, but it covered only about half of the roughly 2,200 aboard. Early boats left well under capacity. More than 1,500 people died, many in water near minus two degrees. Most first class passengers survived; around three quarters of third class did not.\n\nThe liner Carpathia, over 100 kilometres away, steamed through the ice and picked up around 700 survivors. The Californian, much closer, had stopped for the night. Its crew saw rockets but did not understand them, and its only wireless operator had gone to bed."],
    ["What Titanic changed", "The disaster led to international rules on lifeboats, round-the-clock radio watches and ice patrols that still shape shipping.",
      "Inquiries in the United States and Britain led to the first International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea, known as SOLAS, agreed in London in 1914. Ships had to carry lifeboat places for everyone aboard and hold lifeboat drills. Wireless rooms on passenger ships were to be staffed around the clock, so no distress call went unheard.\n\nThe International Ice Patrol was created to track icebergs in the North Atlantic, and it still operates. The wreck lay undiscovered until 1985, when a team led by Robert Ballard and Jean-Louis Michel found it nearly four kilometres down, broken in two. Its slow decay reminds us how a confident age learned humility the hard way."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the truth about the ‘unsinkable’ claim?", ["It was White Star's main advertising slogan","The captain announced it at departure","A trade journal said ‘practically unsinkable’, and the boast grew later","The claim was invented after the wreck was found"], 2, "The strongest versions of the claim spread after the sinking, though real confidence in modern ships mattered."],
    [1, "Why was the iceberg so hard to spot?", ["Fog covered the whole North Atlantic","The sea was calm and moonless, and lookouts had no binoculars","The lookouts had fallen asleep at their posts","The ship's lights had been switched off"], 1, "Flat water and no moon hid the berg until it was close."],
    [2, "Why did Titanic sink even though it could survive some flooding?", ["One enormous gash split the hull in two","Too many compartments flooded, and water spilled over the tops","A hole opened in the upper deck","The rudder snapped off in the collision"], 3, "The design could survive four flooded compartments, but the iceberg opened five or six."],
    [3, "Why did Titanic have so few lifeboats for the people aboard?", ["Lifeboats had not yet been invented","Outdated rules set boat numbers by ship weight, not passengers","Passengers voted against carrying more boats","The owners removed boats to save weight"], 1, "The ship met the old rules, which fell far short of what its 2,200 people needed."],
    [4, "What change helped ensure distress calls would not go unheard?", ["Wireless rooms staffed around the clock on passenger ships","Banning icebergs from the shipping lanes","Fitting passenger ships with much larger funnels","Requiring all liners to travel faster"], 0, "Californian's operator was asleep, so SOLAS required continuous radio watches."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The Titanic's famous ‘unsinkable’ boast is mostly a myth. The real story differs."});

WB.L("dunkirk-evacuation", "history", "beginner", "Dunkirk: the miracle of 1940",
  "In nine days in 1940, an army that seemed doomed was carried home across the English Channel. Britain called it a miracle. Churchill warned that wars are not won by evacuations.",
  [
    ["Trapped, then a pause", "A fast German breakthrough trapped the Allied armies against the sea, and a three-day halt order gave them time to defend Dunkirk.",
      "On 10 May 1940 Germany attacked the Netherlands, Belgium and France. Allied commanders sent their best troops, including most of the British Expeditionary Force, north into Belgium. But German tanks came through the hilly Ardennes, which the French thought too difficult for armour, crossed the Meuse and reached the Channel on 20 May. Hundreds of thousands of Allied soldiers were caught in a shrinking pocket.\n\nOn 24 May the German advance halted, confirmed by Hitler. Tank maintenance, marshy ground, fear of counterattack and Göring's claim that the Luftwaffe could finish the job may all have played a part. The pause lasted around three days, giving the Allies time to strengthen the perimeter, where French troops held out."],
    ["Operation Dynamo", "Operation Dynamo, planned beneath Dover Castle, began hoping to save 45,000 men under terrible conditions.",
      "Vice-Admiral Bertram Ramsay organised the rescue from rooms deep in the cliffs below Dover Castle. The codename, Operation Dynamo, is said to come from a room that once housed an electrical generator. When it began on 26 May, planners hoped to save perhaps 45,000 men in two days.\n\nConditions were terrible. Bombing had wrecked Dunkirk's docks, oil tanks burned and black smoke hung over the town. The beaches sloped so gently that large ships could not get close. Men waited for days on the sand under air attack, in long lines into the sea. Ramsay's staff worked almost without sleep, improvising hour by hour."],
    ["Mole and little ships", "Most soldiers left from a harbour breakwater, while the famous little ships ferried men from the beaches to larger vessels.",
      "The famous image of Dunkirk is a flotilla of pleasure boats, fishing vessels and yachts crossing the Channel. These ‘little ships’ were real and heroic, many crewed by naval reservists or their owners, and vital for carrying men from the shallow beaches to larger ships offshore.\n\nBut most soldiers were taken off from the East Mole, a long concrete and timber breakwater protecting the harbour. It was never designed for ships to tie up against, yet destroyers and passenger ferries came alongside and loaded thousands of men at a time. Many hundreds of vessels took part, from warships to Thames barges. The Royal Navy's destroyers did the heaviest lifting, and several were sunk."],
    ["Fighting overhead and behind", "The RAF's inland air battle and the French rearguard made the escape possible, often unseen by men on the beach.",
      "Soldiers on the beaches were bombed and strafed, and many angrily asked where the Royal Air Force was. In fact the RAF was flying hard, but much of its fighting took place inland or above the cloud, intercepting bombers before they reached the coast. It lost many aircraft and pilots.\n\nOn the ground, defending the perimeter mattered just as much. British units fought delaying actions, and French troops held the line until the end, letting others escape. When the evacuation finished on 4 June, tens of thousands of French soldiers who had protected the beaches were left behind and taken prisoner. Their sacrifice is often forgotten in the British telling."],
    ["A defeat remembered as victory", "About 338,000 troops were rescued, but Churchill insisted that wars are not won by evacuations.",
      "Around 338,000 Allied soldiers were carried to England, including well over 100,000 French troops, many quickly shipped back to fight in France. Against the original hope of 45,000, it seemed a miracle. But many ships were sunk, and the British army abandoned almost all its heavy equipment: tens of thousands of vehicles, hundreds of tanks, guns and ammunition. France signed an armistice on 22 June. Yet Britain kept the core of a trained army, later the backbone of forces in Africa, Italy and Normandy.\n\nOn 4 June Churchill told the Commons that wars are not won by evacuations and called the campaign a colossal military disaster. He ended with his promise to fight on the beaches and never surrender."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did German forces trap the Allied armies?", ["By landing troops from the sea","By besieging Paris for months","By capturing Dunkirk before anyone arrived","By sending tanks through the Ardennes to the Channel"], 3, "The armour crossed terrain the French thought impassable and cut off the armies in the north."],
    [1, "Why was getting men off the beaches so difficult?", ["Wrecked docks and shallow beaches kept big ships away","The Channel was frozen across its whole width","Soldiers refused to board the waiting ships","No ships were available to be sent across"], 0, "Bombing ruined the harbour and shallow beaches kept big ships far offshore."],
    [2, "Where were most soldiers actually taken off?", ["By swimming across to England","From the East Mole, a long harbour breakwater","From a port further along the Belgian coast","From an airfield outside the town"], 1, "The little ships were vital, but most men boarded destroyers and ferries at the mole."],
    [3, "How did the RAF and French troops contribute to the escape?", ["They sank the German fleet in the Channel","They persuaded Hitler to halt the advance","The RAF fought inland while French troops held the line","They ferried the soldiers across the Channel"], 2, "Both fought out of sight of the beaches, and many French troops were captured as a result."],
    [4, "How did Churchill describe the campaign on 4 June?", ["As a complete and glorious victory","As a failure caused by France alone","As a miracle with no losses","As a colossal military disaster, despite the rescue"], 3, "He praised the deliverance but warned that wars are not won by evacuations."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why did German tanks stop outside Dunkirk? Historians still argue about it."});

WB.L("panama-canal", "history", "beginner", "The Panama Canal: cutting a continent in two",
  "For centuries people dreamed of a shortcut between the Atlantic and Pacific. The first attempt ended in disaster. The second succeeded only after doctors worked out what was really killing the workers.",
  [
    ["The dream, the failure", "A canal would save around 13,000 kilometres of sailing, but the first attempt failed because of terrain, a flawed design and disease.",
      "Panama is a narrow strip of land, at its slimmest under 80 kilometres wide. A ship sailing from New York to San Francisco had to go around Cape Horn, a voyage of more than 20,000 kilometres. A canal would cut roughly 13,000 kilometres from it.\n\nIn 1881 a French company led by Ferdinand de Lesseps, hero of the Suez Canal, began digging. He insisted on a sea-level canal with no locks. But Panama was nothing like flat Egyptian desert: mountains, landslides, heavy rains and the flooding Chagres River defeated the plan. Yellow fever and malaria killed workers in huge numbers, around 20,000 during the French years, many of them Caribbean labourers. By 1889 the company was bankrupt, and the half-dug ditches were left to the jungle."],
    ["America steps in", "The United States backed Panama's independence from Colombia in 1903 to secure the canal route.",
      "At the start of the twentieth century, the United States wanted a canal for trade and to move its navy quickly between oceans. President Theodore Roosevelt bought the French company's assets, but Colombia, which then ruled Panama, rejected the treaty terms.\n\nIn November 1903 a revolt in Panama declared independence, and US warships made sure Colombian troops could not land to stop it. Within about two weeks the new government's representative, a Frenchman named Philippe Bunau-Varilla, signed a treaty giving the United States control of a Canal Zone across the country, in perpetuity. Many Panamanians resented the deal from the start, and it became a source of tension in Latin America for decades."],
    ["Defeating the mosquito", "Controlling the mosquitoes that spread yellow fever and malaria made the canal possible.",
      "Work began in 1904, and at first yellow fever returned and workers fled. The breakthrough came from medicine. Building on Carlos Finlay's theory and Walter Reed's experiments in Cuba, doctors had confirmed that yellow fever was spread by mosquitoes, while malaria was carried by a different mosquito.\n\nDr William Gorgas, the chief sanitary officer, launched a vast campaign. Teams drained swamps, spread oil on standing water, fumigated buildings, screened windows and installed piped water so people no longer stored it in open barrels. By 1906 yellow fever had been eliminated from the zone, and malaria deaths fell sharply. It was one of the great public health victories in history, and without it the canal would likely have failed again."],
    ["Locks, lake, workers", "Engineers lifted ships over the land with locks and a huge lake, using Caribbean labour paid under a segregated system.",
      "Chief engineers John Stevens and later George Goethals abandoned the sea-level idea. They dammed the Chagres River at Gatun, creating then the largest artificial lake in the world. Ships climb through giant locks, cross the lake about 26 metres above sea level, then descend through more locks. The hardest work was the Culebra Cut through the mountainous spine of the isthmus, where landslides repeatedly buried months of digging. On 15 August 1914 the cargo ship SS Ancon made the first official transit.\n\nMost labour came from the Caribbean, especially Barbados and Jamaica, doing the most dangerous jobs. White Americans were paid in gold, most other workers in silver, with poorer housing and food. Over 5,000 workers died in the American years."],
    ["Handing back the canal", "Panama took full control of the canal at the end of 1999, but the canal still depends on rain to work.",
      "Resentment of the Canal Zone, a slice of US-run territory cutting through the middle of Panama, grew through the twentieth century. In 1964 riots over flying the Panamanian flag in the zone left more than twenty people dead. In 1977 US President Jimmy Carter and Panama's leader Omar Torrijos signed treaties to transfer the canal. On 31 December 1999 Panama took full control.\n\nSince then it has run the canal profitably and opened larger locks in 2016 to take bigger ships. The canal still depends on rain to fill Gatun Lake, and in 2023 a severe drought forced limits on the number of ships that could pass, a reminder that even great engineering relies on nature."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the French effort to build the canal fail?", ["They ran out of steel for the locks","A sea-level plan, hard terrain and disease overwhelmed it","The United States invaded the work site","Panama refused to allow any digging"], 2, "Landslides, floods and fever defeated de Lesseps' plan."],
    [1, "How did the United States gain control of the canal route?", ["It bought the land outright from Spain","It won a short war against France","Colombia freely gave up the route to it","It backed Panama's break from Colombia, then signed a treaty"], 3, "After Colombia rejected the deal, the US supported a revolt and then secured the zone by treaty."],
    [2, "What breakthrough made construction possible?", ["Controlling the mosquitoes that carried disease","Developing much stronger dynamite","Building faster steamships","Finding a cheaper source of steel"], 0, "Draining swamps and screening homes cut disease, which was the main killer."],
    [3, "How do ships cross the high ground of Panama?", ["Through a long tunnel under the hills","By rising through locks to a lake and back down","On a giant railway across the isthmus","By sailing at sea level the whole way"], 1, "The locks lift ships to Gatun Lake and lower them on the other side."],
    [4, "Why did a drought in 2023 limit ships using the canal?", ["Its locks run on water from rain-fed Gatun Lake","The canal froze during the dry season","Panama closed it for major repairs","There was no fuel left to run the locks"], 0, "Even great engineering depends on nature."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The Panama Canal was only possible after doctors beat a tiny enemy. Which one?"});

WB.L("bismarck-german-unification", "history", "intermediate", "Bismarck and the making of Germany",
  "In 1860 there was no country called Germany, just dozens of separate states. Eleven years later a German empire was proclaimed in a French palace, built by one man's three wars.",
  [
    ["Blood and iron", "Bismarck argued that Germany would be united by force rather than speeches and votes, the supposed mistake of 1848.",
      "After 1815 the German lands formed a loose Confederation of 39 states, from great powers like Austria and Prussia to tiny free cities like Hamburg. Prussia grew rich through industry and the Zollverein, a customs union that excluded Austria. In 1848 an elected parliament in Frankfurt tried to unite Germany, and failed.\n\nIn 1862 King Wilhelm I was stuck in a fight with parliament over funding for a bigger army. He appointed Otto von Bismarck, a ruthless Junker diplomat, who simply carried on collecting taxes without approval. He declared that the great questions of the day would be decided not by speeches and majority votes, but by iron and blood. The phrase came to define his career."],
    ["Three wars in seven years", "Bismarck won short wars against Denmark, Austria and France by isolating each enemy first.",
      "Bismarck's genius lay in fighting one enemy at a time, ensuring no other power joined in. In 1864 Prussia and Austria together defeated Denmark over Schleswig and Holstein. Bismarck then used disputes over running those duchies to provoke Austria.\n\nIn 1866 the Prussian army, using railways, telegraphs and fast-firing breech-loading rifles, crushed Austria at Königgrätz in a war lasting only weeks. Bismarck insisted on mild terms so Austria would not seek revenge, but it was pushed out of German affairs. Prussia annexed several states and formed the North German Confederation. Only the southern states, including Bavaria, remained outside. Bismarck believed a war with France would bring them in."],
    ["The Ems Telegram and Versailles", "Bismarck edited a royal telegram to provoke France into war, then saw the German Empire proclaimed at Versailles.",
      "In 1870 a relative of the Prussian king was offered the Spanish throne. France protested and it was withdrawn, but France pressed further, demanding at Ems a promise it would never be renewed. Wilhelm politely declined and telegraphed Bismarck. Bismarck shortened the message so the king seemed to have curtly snubbed the ambassador. The French public was outraged and France declared war, appearing the aggressor. The southern states, bound by defensive alliances, joined Prussia.\n\nAt Sedan Napoleon III was captured. On 18 January 1871, with Paris besieged, Wilhelm I was proclaimed Kaiser in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, a deliberate humiliation. France paid an indemnity and gave up Alsace and much of Lorraine."],
    ["Bismarck at home", "Bismarck repressed Catholics and socialists, yet created the world's first national social insurance to win workers over.",
      "As chancellor, Bismarck saw enemies of the new empire everywhere. In the 1870s he waged the Kulturkampf against the power of the Catholic Church, which backfired by strengthening Catholic political loyalty. He then turned on the socialist movement, banning its organisations from 1878.\n\nYet alongside repression came a remarkable innovation. To win workers away from socialism, he introduced national health insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884, and old age and disability pensions in 1889. Workers, employers and the state shared the costs. It was the first system of its kind in the world and became a model for many other countries. His motives were conservative, but the legacy proved lasting."],
    ["The juggler and his fall", "Bismarck kept the peace through a web of alliances that his successors let fall apart.",
      "After 1871 Bismarck declared Germany satisfied. His aim was to prevent a war that could destroy what he had built, especially a two-front war against France and Russia. He presented himself as an honest broker at the Congress of Berlin in 1878, and wove a network of treaties: the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary in 1879, later the Triple Alliance with Italy, and a secret Reinsurance Treaty with Russia in 1887.\n\nIn 1888 the young Wilhelm II came to the throne and clashed with the old chancellor, who was forced to resign in 1890. The treaty with Russia was not renewed, and Russia soon allied with France. The isolation Bismarck feared was beginning to take shape, setting the stage for 1914."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Bismarck mean by ‘iron and blood’?", ["That Germany needed steel mills","That parliaments should decide through long debate","That force, not speeches and votes, would settle Germany's future","That Austria should lead unification"], 2, "He believed the 1848 parliamentarians had failed because they relied on words, not power."],
    [1, "What made Bismarck's three wars so effective?", ["He fought all his enemies at the same time","He relied almost entirely on naval power","He kept his army away from railways","He isolated each enemy and fought short wars"], 3, "One enemy at a time, with no outside help, kept the wars quick and decisive."],
    [2, "What did Bismarck do with the Ems Telegram?", ["He destroyed it before it reached anyone","He edited it so the king seemed to insult France","He sent it to Russia as a warning","He had it translated into French for the press"], 1, "The edit inflamed French opinion, so France appeared to be the aggressor."],
    [3, "Why did Bismarck introduce national social insurance?", ["To win workers away from socialism","Because Austria demanded it","To pay for the army","To please the Catholic Church"], 0, "His motives were conservative, but he created the world's first system of its kind."],
    [4, "What happened after Bismarck was forced out in 1890?", ["Germany disbanded its army soon after","The Russia treaty lapsed, and Russia turned to France","Germany joined France against Austria","Wilhelm II abdicated within a year"], 1, "The isolation he had feared began to take shape."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Bismarck edited one telegram and helped start a war. Here's how."});

WB.L("revolutions-of-1848", "history", "intermediate", "1848: the year Europe caught fire",
  "In a few weeks of 1848, revolutions broke out in dozens of cities across Europe. Kings fled, ministers resigned and constitutions were promised. Within two years almost all of it had been undone.",
  [
    ["A continent under pressure", "Repressive rule, new ideas and a hunger crisis combined to make Europe ready to explode in 1848.",
      "After 1815, Europe's great powers tried to restore order under conservative monarchs. Austria's Prince Metternich symbolised the system of censorship, secret police and suspicion of change. Beneath the surface pressures were building. Liberals wanted constitutions, elected parliaments and a free press. Nationalists wanted peoples such as Germans, Italians, Hungarians and Poles to govern themselves. Industrialisation was creating a new urban working class in grim conditions.\n\nThen, in the mid 1840s, potato blight and poor grain harvests sent food prices soaring, followed by a financial crisis and mass unemployment in 1847. Hungry, angry crowds and frustrated middle class reformers were a combustible mix."],
    ["The springtime of peoples", "The overthrow of France's king in February 1848 set off revolutions in city after city within weeks.",
      "A revolt in Palermo came first, in January, but Paris ignited the continent. King Louis-Philippe banned political banquets used by reformers. In February protests turned into street fighting, soldiers fired on a crowd, and within days the king fled to England. The Second Republic gave all adult men the vote, abolished slavery in French colonies and created national workshops for the unemployed.\n\nNews travelled fast by rail, telegraph and newspaper. In March Metternich resigned and fled Vienna. In Berlin, after deadly barricade fighting, King Frederick William IV promised a constitution. Milan drove out the Austrians, and Hungary under Lajos Kossuth won sweeping self-government. In weeks, governments that seemed immovable gave way to demands that a year earlier meant prison."],
    ["The Frankfurt Parliament", "German liberals elected a national parliament to unite Germany, but it had no army, no money and no ruler willing to accept its crown.",
      "In May 1848 an elected assembly met in St Paul's Church in Frankfurt to create a united, constitutional Germany. Its members were mostly lawyers, professors and civil servants. They debated long and earnestly, drawing up basic rights including freedom of speech and equality before the law.\n\nBut they faced hard questions. Should Germany include German-speaking Austria and its many non-German peoples? And the parliament had no army or money of its own. In 1849 it offered the crown of a united Germany to Frederick William IV of Prussia. He refused a crown offered by an elected assembly rather than by fellow princes. The parliament soon dissolved, and its remnant was dispersed by troops."],
    ["Why the revolutions failed", "Revolutionaries divided among themselves while the armies stayed loyal to the old rulers.",
      "The revolutions failed for several connected reasons. Middle class liberals wanted constitutions and order, while workers wanted jobs and social change, and the two soon feared each other. In Paris, closing the national workshops sparked the bloody June Days uprising, crushed by the army with thousands killed.\n\nNationalities quarrelled too: Hungarians demanded freedom from Austria while denying it to Croats and Romanians within Hungary. Above all, the armies mostly stayed loyal to their monarchs. In late 1848 Austrian troops retook Vienna, and young Franz Joseph became emperor. In 1849 Russian troops helped crush Hungary. In France, voters elected Louis-Napoleon Bonaparte president, and in 1851 he seized power and soon declared himself emperor."],
    ["What survived", "Though defeated, 1848 freed Austria's peasants, spread constitutions and sent exiled Forty-Eighters across the world.",
      "Some changes endured. In the Austrian Empire, peasants were freed from compulsory labour owed to landlords, and this was never reversed. Prussia kept a limited constitution and parliament. Denmark became a constitutional monarchy in 1849, the Netherlands liberalised its constitution, and Switzerland adopted a federal constitution. Piedmont-Sardinia kept its constitution and later led Italian unification. Rulers learned that ignoring public opinion was dangerous, and increasingly used nationalism for their own ends, as Bismarck later did.\n\nThousands of exiled Germans, the Forty-Eighters, emigrated to the United States, some later fighting for the Union. Britain's Chartists held a huge London meeting in April 1848 but fizzled out. The Communist Manifesto appeared in February 1848 but had little influence that year."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What economic troubles helped trigger the revolutions?", ["A gold rush that drained workers from farms","A sudden boom in wages and employment","Crop failures, high food prices and a financial crisis","The abolition of the slave trade"], 2, "Hunger and unemployment turned discontent into revolt."],
    [1, "Why did the Paris revolution of February 1848 matter so much?", ["It ended every monarchy in Europe at once","It showed that a king could be overthrown, inspiring others","It was won by foreign armies","It gave women the vote across Europe"], 1, "News travelled fast, and liberals elsewhere concluded that if Paris could do it, so could they."],
    [2, "Why did the Frankfurt Parliament fail to unite Germany?", ["It voted against any form of unity at all","It had no army or money, and Prussia's king refused its crown","France invaded Frankfurt and shut it down","Its members had never been elected by anyone"], 1, "Without power of its own, it relied on rulers who then rejected it."],
    [3, "Which factor most helped the old rulers regain control?", ["Railways stopped working","The armies mostly stayed loyal to their monarchs","Britain invaded Europe","The revolutionaries lost every election"], 1, "Divided revolutionaries could not match loyal armies."],
    [4, "Which change in the Austrian Empire outlasted the revolutions?", ["A republic ruled by elected leaders","Independence for Hungary from Austria","The end of compulsory peasant labour for landlords","Votes for every adult in the empire"], 2, "Peasants stayed free of the old labour dues even after the revolution was crushed."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In 1848 kings fell across Europe in weeks. Why did so little of it last?"});

WB.L("vietnam-war-australia", "history", "intermediate", "Australia's Vietnam War",
  "It was Australia's longest war of the twentieth century and its most divisive. Young men were chosen for it by a lottery of birthdays, and many came home to silence.",
  [
    ["Why Australia went", "Fear of communism spreading through Asia, and the wish to keep the United States close, drew Australia into Vietnam.",
      "After the Second World War, Australian leaders feared communism in Asia, especially after China's communist victory in 1949 and the Korean War. The domino theory held that if one country fell, its neighbours would follow until the threat reached Australia. Vietnam was divided in 1954 into a communist North and non-communist South, and an insurgency grew in the South. Australia also wanted to strengthen its alliance with the United States, formalised in the ANZUS treaty of 1951. In 1962 it sent thirty military advisers.\n\nIn April 1965 Prime Minister Robert Menzies announced an infantry battalion, saying South Vietnam had requested help, a claim later questioned. From 1966 the 1st Australian Task Force at Nui Dat focused on patient jungle patrolling."],
    ["The birthday ballot", "National service selected twenty-year-old men by drawing birth dates, and conscripts could be sent to Vietnam.",
      "In 1964 the government introduced national service. Twenty-year-old men had to register, and a lottery drew birth dates from a barrel. If yours was drawn, you could be called up for two years. From 1965 these conscripts, often called nashos, could be sent overseas, including to Vietnam.\n\nAround 15,000 national servicemen served there, and some were killed. The randomness troubled many Australians. A young man's future, and perhaps his life, could depend on whether a marble bearing his birth date came out of a barrel. Some refused to register or serve, and a few were jailed. The ballot made the war deeply personal for families across the country."],
    ["Long Tan", "In August 1966 a single Australian company held off a much larger enemy force in a rubber plantation.",
      "On 18 August 1966, D Company of the 6th Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment, about 105 men, was patrolling near Nui Dat in a rubber plantation called Long Tan. In a tropical downpour they met a far larger force of Viet Cong and North Vietnamese troops.\n\nFor several hours the Australians, with three New Zealand artillery observers, fought off repeated attacks, helped by intense artillery fire from the base and an ammunition drop by helicopter. As darkness fell, armoured personnel carriers arrived and the attackers withdrew. Eighteen Australians were killed. Enemy losses were far higher, though exact numbers are disputed. Long Tan became the best-known Australian action of the war, and 18 August is now Vietnam Veterans Day."],
    ["A divided country", "As the war dragged on without victory, opposition grew into some of the largest protests in Australian history.",
      "At first many Australians supported the war, but doubts grew as it dragged on, particularly after the Tet Offensive of 1968 showed the enemy was far from beaten. Television brought images of the fighting into lounge rooms. Groups such as Save Our Sons, formed by mothers of possible conscripts, campaigned against the ballot.\n\nIn May 1970 the Moratorium marches brought huge crowds into cities around Australia. In Melbourne, led by Labor politician Jim Cairns, estimates ranged from 70,000 to 100,000. Supporters of the war saw protesters as disloyal; many protesters saw the government sending young men to die for an unwinnable cause. The divide split families, churches and friendships."],
    ["Withdrawal and coming home", "Australian troops withdrew by 1972, but many veterans came home to indifference and were recognised only years later.",
      "As the United States reduced its forces, Australia followed. The task force left Phuoc Tuy in late 1971. In December 1972 the Whitlam government ended conscription and freed men jailed for refusing to serve. Almost 60,000 Australians served and around 520 died. Vietnamese deaths ran into the millions. Saigon fell in April 1975, and Australia later resettled tens of thousands of Vietnamese refugees.\n\nMost veterans came home individually, often quietly, expected to slip back into ordinary life. Some were abused by protesters, and some older veterans' groups were slow to welcome them. Many struggled with post-traumatic stress, and concerns grew about Agent Orange, investigated by a Royal Commission in the 1980s. In October 1987 tens of thousands marched in Sydney's Welcome Home parade, and a national memorial opened in Canberra in 1992."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the domino theory?", ["A plan to build railways across Asia","A strategy for winning Australian elections","The idea that falling countries would drag their neighbours with them","A theory about growth in Asian economies"], 2, "Australian leaders feared communism would spread south until it reached Australia."],
    [1, "How were national servicemen selected?", ["By exam results","By height and weight","By volunteering only","By a ballot of birth dates"], 3, "A lottery of birthdays decided which twenty-year-olds were called up."],
    [2, "How did the outnumbered Australians hold on at Long Tan?", ["Tanks reached them within a few minutes","Artillery and an ammunition drop sustained them until carriers came","The enemy withdrew at the very first contact","Radar guided every shot the Australians fired"], 1, "Support from the base kept them fighting until armoured carriers arrived."],
    [3, "Why did support for the war erode?", ["Conscription was abolished in 1960","Television was banned from the battlefield","After Tet showed the enemy undefeated, television brought the war home","The United States demanded Australian withdrawal"], 2, "Doubts grew as victory seemed out of reach."],
    [4, "What did many veterans face when they returned?", ["A national parade on the day they landed","Indifference or hostility, with recognition coming years later","Immediate pensions and honours for all","Official bans on talking about their service"], 1, "Recognition came slowly, culminating in the 1987 Welcome Home parade."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Australia chose conscripts by drawing birthdays from a barrel. Here's how it worked."});

WB.L("apartheid-south-africa", "history", "intermediate", "Apartheid: rise and fall",
  "For more than four decades, South Africa's laws sorted every person by race and decided where they could live, work, love and vote. Then, against most predictions, the system was dismantled without the all-out civil war many had feared.",
  [
    ["Building apartheid", "After 1948, South Africa's government turned long-standing discrimination into a total legal system that classified everyone by race.",
      "Discrimination long predated apartheid. When the Union of South Africa formed in 1910, most black people were denied the vote. The Natives Land Act of 1913 restricted black land ownership, and pass laws controlled travel and work. In 1912 black leaders founded what became the African National Congress. The word apartheid, Afrikaans for apartness, entered politics in the 1940s.\n\nIn 1948 the National Party won office and built the system. The Population Registration Act of 1950 classified everyone as white, coloured, Indian or black. Cross-racial marriage was banned. The Group Areas Act created separate residential areas, and communities such as District Six and Sophiatown were bulldozed. Separate and unequal schools, hospitals and beaches followed, and Bantu Education prepared black children for labouring lives."],
    ["The homelands", "Apartheid's planners tried to strip black South Africans of citizenship by assigning them to separate ‘homelands’.",
      "The most ambitious part of apartheid was the homeland system. The government divided black South Africans into ethnic groups and assigned each to a territory, or Bantustan, often scattered fragments of poor land. The aim was to declare these homelands independent, so black people would become citizens of them rather than of South Africa, leaving a white-ruled state with no black citizens at all.\n\nFour homelands, including Transkei, were declared independent, though almost no other country recognised them. Millions were forcibly moved there in the 1960s and 1970s, often to places with few jobs or services. Workers who needed city jobs became migrants in their own country, living in hostels far from their families."],
    ["Resistance and repression", "Peaceful protest was met with bullets and bans, pushing the resistance towards armed struggle and a new generation of youth revolt.",
      "Through the 1950s the ANC led nonviolent campaigns and in 1955 adopted the Freedom Charter. On 21 March 1960 at Sharpeville, police opened fire on a pass-law protest, killing 69 people. The ANC was banned. Nelson Mandela and others concluded that peaceful protest was failing, and in 1961 Umkhonto we Sizwe began sabotage. After the Rivonia trial in 1964 Mandela was sentenced to life.\n\nOn 16 June 1976 thousands of Soweto students marched against being taught some subjects in Afrikaans. Police opened fire, and a photograph of dying twelve-year-old Hector Pieterson travelled the world. Hundreds were killed in the unrest. Steve Biko's Black Consciousness movement urged pride; he died in police custody in 1977. In the 1980s protests and boycotts led to states of emergency."],
    ["The world turns away", "Sanctions and boycotts isolated South Africa, though historians debate how much they mattered compared with internal resistance.",
      "International pressure grew steadily. The United Nations condemned apartheid, and many countries imposed trade, arms and financial sanctions, especially in the 1980s. Banks refused new loans and companies pulled out, damaging an already struggling economy.\n\nSport mattered deeply to many white South Africans, and sporting boycotts struck at their sense of belonging in the world. South Africa was excluded from the Olympics for decades. In Australia, a 1971 tour by the Springbok rugby team sparked large protests and clashes, and later governments of both major parties took strong anti-apartheid positions in the Commonwealth. Historians debate how much sanctions alone forced change compared with internal resistance and economic decline, but together they made apartheid increasingly costly to keep."],
    ["Release and 1994", "Despite violence, negotiations led to South Africa's first fully democratic election in 1994 and Mandela's presidency.",
      "By the late 1980s many in government realised apartheid could not survive. On 2 February 1990 President F. W. de Klerk announced the ANC would be legalised. On 11 February Nelson Mandela walked out of prison after 27 years. Negotiations were interrupted by violence that killed thousands. In 1993 the assassination of ANC leader Chris Hani brought the country to the brink, but Mandela's appeal for calm held. He and de Klerk shared the Nobel Peace Prize.\n\nOn 27 April 1994 South Africans of every race voted together for the first time, many queueing for hours. The ANC won about 62 per cent, and Mandela became president of a government of national unity. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission heard testimony. Apartheid law was gone, but its economic legacy of deep inequality remains."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Population Registration Act of 1950 do?", ["Gave every adult the vote","Created a national register of farms","Classified every South African into a racial group","Abolished the pass laws"], 2, "Racial classification was the foundation for the rest of the apartheid system."],
    [1, "What was the goal of the homeland system?", ["To give black South Africans much more farmland","To strip black South Africans of their South African citizenship","To build new cities shared by people of all races","To end the pass laws within a generation"], 1, "Independent homelands would have left a white-ruled state with no black citizens."],
    [2, "Why did leaders such as Mandela turn to armed struggle?", ["They had just won a national election","Peaceful protest was met with bullets and bans at Sharpeville","The United Nations had formally ordered it","Sanctions had already failed to have any effect"], 1, "After the ANC was banned, they concluded peaceful protest alone was failing."],
    [3, "What do historians say about sanctions and boycotts?", ["They had almost no effect on South Africa","They alone ended apartheid in 1990","They worked only in sport and in Australia","They added to internal resistance and economic decline"], 3, "Historians debate sanctions' share, but all pressures together made apartheid costly to keep."],
    [4, "What helped South Africa avoid civil war during the transition?", ["Foreign troops took control of the country","Negotiations, and Mandela's call for calm after Hani's killing","The army seized power for several years","Apartheid laws were kept in place by agreement"], 1, "Tense negotiations continued despite violence, and calm held."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A system of racial laws ended in 1994 without the civil war many predicted. Why?"});

WB.L("maos-china", "history", "advanced", "Mao's China: the Great Leap and the Cultural Revolution",
  "Mao Zedong set out to transform China into a modern socialist power within a generation. Two of his campaigns caused one of the deadliest famines in history and a decade of political terror.",
  [
    ["Victory, then a trap", "After winning power in 1949, Mao remade society by force, then punished the people who criticised him.",
      "On 1 October 1949, Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic of China after the Communists beat Chiang Kai-shek's Nationalists, who retreated to Taiwan. Many welcomed peace after decades of invasion, warlords and civil war. Land reform gave land to peasants through public ‘struggle sessions’ in which many landlords were killed. Estimates of deaths in these campaigns range from several hundred thousand to several million. Private businesses were nationalised.\n\nIn 1956 Mao invited criticism: ‘let a hundred flowers bloom’. When it turned to the party's right to rule, he reversed course. The 1957 Anti-Rightist Campaign labelled about 550,000 people, many of them teachers, writers and scientists, as ‘rightists’, and many were sent to labour camps. Honest criticism became dangerous, and that silence had disastrous consequences."],
    ["The Great Leap Forward", "From 1958, Mao tried to turn China into an industrial power almost overnight through mass mobilisation.",
      "In 1958 Mao launched the Great Leap Forward, aiming to overtake Britain in steel production within about fifteen years and to prove that mass enthusiasm could beat Soviet-style caution. Hundreds of millions of peasants were organised into vast people's communes, where private plots were abolished and families ate in communal canteens.\n\nMillions of backyard furnaces consumed pots, pans and tools, and much of the steel they made was useless. Farmers were pulled from the fields to work on furnaces and dams. Pseudo-scientific methods such as very close planting and deep ploughing were imposed. A campaign against the Four Pests killed huge numbers of sparrows, and crop-eating insects flourished."],
    ["Lies, quotas and famine", "Inflated harvest reports led the state to seize grain that villagers needed, causing one of history's deadliest famines.",
      "Local officials, terrified of being labelled rightists, reported fantastic harvests that never existed. The state took grain based on those false figures, leaving many villages with little or nothing to eat, while China continued to export grain. In 1959 Defence Minister Peng Dehuai privately criticised the Leap in a letter to Mao. Mao purged him, and the campaign was pushed even harder.\n\nThe famine of 1959 to 1961 followed. Estimates of excess deaths range from around fifteen million to more than forty million, and many demographers put it near thirty million. It was largely caused by policy. By the early 1960s, Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping quietly allowed private plots and markets again, and food production slowly recovered."],
    ["The Cultural Revolution", "In 1966 Mao unleashed young Red Guards against officials, teachers and tradition, and turned on his own colleagues.",
      "Mao stepped back from running the economy but grew suspicious. He believed the party was becoming a privileged elite, that the Soviet Union showed how a revolution could decay, and he resented his lost authority. His cult of personality grew, helped by Defence Minister Lin Biao, whose army published the Little Red Book.\n\nIn 1966 Mao launched the Cultural Revolution, calling on young people to attack ‘bourgeois’ influences. Red Guards waved the Little Red Book at huge Tiananmen Square rallies. They humiliated, beat and sometimes killed teachers and officials, and destroyed books, temples and historic sites in a campaign against the ‘Four Olds’: old customs, culture, habits and ideas. Liu Shaoqi was purged and died in custody in 1969. Deng Xiaoping was sent to work in a tractor factory. Rival factions fought, and the army restored order."],
    ["Sent down, and after Mao", "Political violence lasted a decade, and China's leaders later gave a carefully limited verdict on Mao's rule.",
      "From 1968 Mao sent an estimated sixteen to seventeen million urban youths ‘down to the countryside’ to learn from peasants. Many lost their education, an experience that shaped a generation, including Xi Jinping. In 1971 Lin Biao, Mao's chosen successor, died in a plane crash in Mongolia after apparently fleeing a failed plot, though details remain murky. Estimates of deaths in the Cultural Revolution range from hundreds of thousands to two million or more.\n\nMao died on 9 September 1976, and within a month the Gang of Four, including his widow Jiang Qing, were arrested. Deng Xiaoping then opened China to markets. A 1981 party resolution called the Cultural Revolution a catastrophe but said Mao's contributions outweighed his errors. Research remains restricted, so estimates still vary."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What lesson did people take from the Anti-Rightist Campaign?", ["Open criticism of the party was dangerous","Intellectuals would now run policy","Landlords could safely return","Foreign advice should be sought"], 0, "Punishing those who spoke up taught everyone to stay silent, which proved disastrous later."],
    [1, "Why did the Great Leap Forward's steel drive and farming methods backfire?", ["Backyard furnaces made high-quality steel cheaply","Communes raised private plots for every family","Killing sparrows helped crops to flourish","Farmers left the fields and much of the steel was useless"], 3, "Labour was pulled from farming into poor-quality projects, and harmful methods were imposed from above."],
    [2, "How did false harvest reports help cause the famine?", ["Farmers were told to sell grain at a loss","Officials hid grain to share it later","The state seized grain based on figures that were too high","Imports were cut after the reports"], 2, "Grain taken on the basis of imaginary harvests left villages with too little to eat."],
    [3, "What did Mao call on young people to do in 1966?", ["Rebuild the economy under local officials","Return quietly to their classrooms","Attack ‘bourgeois’ influences within the party and society","Defend the party's older leaders"], 2, "The Cultural Revolution turned Red Guards against officials, teachers and tradition."],
    [4, "Why do estimates of the Cultural Revolution's human toll vary so widely?", ["The records were published in full and disputed","Open research is restricted and archives are hard to access","The campaign lasted only a few months","Most of those affected left China"], 1, "Limited access to archives and research leaves historians working with uncertain evidence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Officials reported harvests that never existed. What followed was sobering."});

